 tackled the  digital and green transitions  are central when building a more  resilient and sustainable european union  but this transition won't be a success  if it's not made in a socially  positive manner that leaves no one  behind  the european green deal is the eu  solution to these challenges  the idea is to transform our system in  order to win the fight against climate  change  but how will this affect us  we have about a decade that's what  scientists tell us  to change dramatically or our planet  will not be  capable to cope with the rhythm in which  we are consuming the resources so the  way we are using  fresh water the way we are fishing the  way we are eating and obviously the way  we are using fossil fuels to basically  sustain almost our all of our way of  living so that has to change that is why  we want  not just to use more renewable energy  but to consume less energy  to change the way we transport goods or  people around the world  at the same time we understand fully  that it's not just about going into this  direction of greening of the  carbonization but it's also taking in  account  the consequences on people's lives on  workers lives on their jobs  we need to put a red heart a social  dimension into the green deal  with concrete policies training people  so that they can get the new jobs the  new opportunities that will come  the social ecological crisis is already  happening  inequalities are rising at rocket speed  the world's richest one percent  have more than twice as much wealth as  6.9 billion people  and our planet is in danger we need to  limit global warming by at least 2  degrees celsius by 2050  so we need to change the economy and  that's what the european green deal is  all about  but that leaves a few questions hanging  will workers have to change sectors  and if so will they be retrained will we  lose jobs as a result of  artificial intelligence and robots  what role is there for social security  schemes in this digital world  the reality of today is quite different  than it was in the 19th century  so the question is has the european  union done anything  to protect social rights  crisis  [Music]  for instance let's say let's get rid of  the youth unemployment  in the next 10 years let's end the  difference in pay between men  and women i think our fight should be  the fight for  social targets mandatory and  legally enforceable social rights touch  on many different aspects  for example youth unemployment or  teleworking and the right to disconnect  or gender pay gap the fact that men and  women are still not paid the same for  the same job  people who regularly work from home  are twice as likely to work more than  the  maximum stipulated working hours workers  deserve  the right to disconnect for us the youth  guarantee is an important instrument to  guarantee  social rights for young people now it  needs to be stronger  with more money it needs to get to those  young people that are mostly need  and especially we need to be very hard  on  unpaid internships we want to ban and  fed internships  since the beginning of the copy 19  pandemic women have been hit harder  as in all crises women's economical  situations  risk being harder hit in the long term  this is a battle we have to fight on  many fronts  for women and for gender equality social  rights have a deep impact on what our  lives  look like but today we are facing  unprecedented changes  how can people continue to be protected  when the world is moving so fast  in the beginning of this episode i told  the story of a delivery biker  how can we protect him i think it's  very clear that we can help him with one  solution  and that's our snd goal and that's  called  a reversal of the burden of proof  reversing the burden of proof means  assuming that the employment  relationship  between worker and digital platform  provides a full social and workers  rights  and it is up to the platforms to  demonstrate if that's not the case  because they say flexibility  entrepreneurship  extra income but what it comes down to  is a modern form of slavery  if uber drivers do not get any customers  they are stuck with the bill for the gas  if the livery bikers have an accident  they will be without sickbay  some of the richest companies on this  planet outsource their business risk  onto the workers and onto the taxpayers  to change the game of the gig economy  all platform workers must be considered  as employees  you have this huge transition towards  the carbonization towards the green  society  but at the same time to a digital  society we want a fair recovery  that means that those that are polluting  have to pay for this recovery those that  are the big companies that have avoided  to pay taxes in the past because they  use the loopholes in this in that  country  they will have to pay also for this  recovery and if we get those resources  and we direct them  towards this transition and towards the  the low-income families the low-income  households  then we are also creating a fairer  society  [Music]  the porto social summit on the 7th of  may 2021  will provide an opportunity for heads of  state ngos  unions and civil society to put social  rights back on top of the eu's agenda
 

